THIS IS NEWS - October 2026
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
I've beenn working on a big new feature set this month, the chain data
object plus autochain_create directive - it's another form of grouping,
in fact the first of two planned forms of grouping working alongside
the existing autogroup/group.

The primary motivator for this is wcnt's lack of nested grouping. I
decided implemting another two levels of grouping would be easier
than recursive grouping, plus a definite improvement on just a single
level.

Chaining is to be considered at the bottom of the heirarchy of what
will become 3 different forms of grouping.

It's taken a very different direction to the standard group, and in
doing so it's opened up capabilities I didn't envisage when I originally
came up with the idea.

Firstly its primary difference is that it holds a copy of the chained
modules and their connections, within itself. These are distinct from
those modules in regular user space. They're inaccessible for use by
input/output connections.

Similarly to group, the chain modifies the usernames of the modules in
regular user space, with its own distinct seperator/signifier (~ tilde
symbol).

However, unlike group, when a chain is duplicated, it isn't the
modules in regular user space which are copied, it is the chain's
internal copy. It works a bit like copy and paste.

This is where my choice of the term chain almost proves prescient. The
chain works at another level of nesting to grouping, so I wanted it to
be able to form a chain within an autogroup, then reproduce that chain
outside of the autogroup. That meant the modules internal to the chain
had to work independently of the group, by losing their group info (with
exceptions). The logical step was to apply that to the chain the modules
were themselves a part of, losing too the chain info component of their
username.

Consequentally I found the chain could *pull* modules out of groups and
other chains. Initially that seemed problematic, but I decided to allow
it, especially if that behaviour could be turned off; if the chain could
be set to copy_only.

Using the chain data object with copy_only set means the user space
modules that the chain duplicates internally, aren't touched at all by
username changes. They remain as they are. The chain does a 'copy only'.

It didn't initially make sense to me to add this as a directive to
change the behaviour of the autochain_create directive - because the
directive can't pull modules from outside of itself... but wait, it
does make sense if you interpret 'copy only' as meaning 'don't create'.

It's a basic form of template for module groups.

Lastly, with all this new capability for grouping I wondered what might
happen if a copier copied a chain inside an autochain. I settled on
an idea which chaining had taken precedent over - flattening a group.
This means a copy of a chain can be made inside an autochain. The
chain name is merged with the module name to make room for the autochain
name to replace it as its chain name component.

It's a rudimentary form of nested grouping.

THIS THIS IS THE NEWS - May-July 2026 - THE NEWS IS THIS THIS
-------------------------------------------------------------
  (Urrrrrgh, where am I? Wait... Wut!?!? It's 2026!?!?)

New features for Wav Composer Not Toilet!

New features summary:
    * Autogrouping - group modules as they're created.

    * Entry points - a quicker way to connect sequencer outputs to
      to the inputs of multiple modules at once.

    * Nameless data objects - data objects which are not referenced
      by name no longer require naming.

Autogrouping
------------
Autogrouping introduces an easier way of adding modules to a group.
Instead of the creation of modules and then grouping as a two step
process, autogrouping groups the modules as they're created.

    * Simpler syntax than the group data object.
    * Less work to keep track of modules which need to be added to
      (or removed from) a group.
    * Quicker and easier to introduce to or remove grouping from a file
    * Allows further modules to be added to existing groups.

The concept of directives was introduced to make this happen, with three
for the autogrouping process: autogroup_create, autogroup_add, and
autogroup_stop. The name of the group must be specified alongside each
of them.

    // example:
    autogroup_create group1
        constant one value 1.0 one
    autogroup_stop group1

    // something else here...

    autogroup_add
    group1
        constant_freq cf value 440.0 cf

        multiplier
        m
            in_signal1 one out_output
            in_signal2 cf out_output
        m
    group1

autogroup_stop is optional to explicilty specify, but must be triggered
by using the name of the group instead, which makes the syntax of
autogroup definitions in keeping with modules and data objects.

See tests/autogroup.wc for a simple example, and examples/bounce2.wc
for more a complex examples.

In some files, or through your own usage you might come across data
objects within an autogroup section. These are not grouped but do work
with the autogroup in that, for instance, you wanted to edit group1.cf,
doing so within the autogroup you would only need specify cf as the
module name and not the full group qualified name (group1.cf).


Entry Points
------------
The concept of entry points arises from the wish to create a voice part
in a file which can then be reused by multiple other files. Modules in
the voice part connect to the entry point, and then when that voice part
is used, the entry point is connected up appropriately.

For multi-layered voice parts, this concept dramatically reduces the
number of inputs requiring reconnection.

It's not exactly a new feature, the pieces were in place - wcnt_trigger
and wcnt_signal modules... No! No! No! No!

It's not exactly a new feature, the piece is already in place - the
wcnt_note module. The wcnt_note has everything we need all in one place,
which really shouldn't come as a suprise to me as I created it for
the seq_router module.

However, due to the way these modules work (wcnt_trigger actually does
nothing at all in the main processing loop), and the fact I had never
thought to use them in this way, the way the logic of how connections
are formed didn't accommodate all this, which created an error saying
outputs didn't exist when they do!

The fix was to postpone these connections until... they were ready.

---jwm/May 2026

